 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
 A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, R3's two targeting parts substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the pooled reduction in the attacker's gain, and in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3) the window changes the gain by \site{abs_dp}. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}).
+Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, R3's two targeting parts substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the pooled reduction in the attacker's gain, and in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3) the window changes the gain by \site{abs_dp}, which is within search error. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}). An exploratory follow-up after the report, specified after these outcomes were seen, finds that within the D2 family most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap, and the C$^*$ minus R3 difference, disappear when the attacker cannot replace its identity, so R3's measured advantage lies in the replacement channel.
 The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
 % END-EV2
 \end{abstract}
@@ -37,7 +37,7 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 
 \paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a measured cost in that provider's profit per round: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
 % BEGIN-EV2
-\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
+\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets, while under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on every market. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
 % END-EV2
 
 \section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
@@ -64,7 +64,7 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 
 \paragraph{Admissible rules.} A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought, its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only consequences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity's receipts as a bond; and allocation priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.
 
-\paragraph{Benchmark and metrics.} One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly at full effort with truthful claims; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best surplus $S^*$, the focal provider's \emph{gain} is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected truthful reference, divided by $S^*/N$. \emph{Gross efficiency} is $(W + \text{audit cost})/S^*$, with $W$ the realised social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is $W/S^*$. Gain is a difference of two searched values, so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The second test adds two objectives at its headline market: welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker's reference on the training seeds (W1), whose profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95\% lower bound on the evaluation seeds is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain $G_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and by the worst member's mean gain $G_{\min}$, both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results say nothing about coalition stability.
