 \section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
+\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:results}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
 
 \paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
