Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule
Ömer Demirel
Draft 9, November 2026

Abstract
A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery,
costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the
uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing,
because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals
whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a
new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered
test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C∗ , at matched budget and sanctions on
six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash. Four findings. First, pooled over
the six markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin of
±0.05 (C∗ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: 0.021); exploratory t intervals over the six market
differences are [−0.013, 0.054] with the frozen searches, [0.014, 0.045] with the doubled searches,
and [0.009, 0.052] with each rule’s strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size,
and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second
attack family repeated the first family’s winner in 10 of 12 cells. Second, R3’s two targeting
parts substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the pooled reduction in the
attacker’s gain, and in R3’s own comparison (with and without the priority window, under
D2+H3) the window changes the gain by −0.003, which is within search error. Third, at the
headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits 0.917 of a replacement
identity’s jobs in its second ten rounds, C∗ 0.161 in the same bin, and the replacement’s profit
per round under R3 differs from that under C∗ by a fraction of −0.044. Fourth, under D2+H3,
R3 lowers the attacker’s gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered
replication passes on 3 of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules’ gross
efficiency to about 0.840 while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain −0.094).
An exploratory follow-up after the report, specified after these outcomes were seen, finds that
within the D2 family most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap, and the C∗ minus R3 difference,
disappear when the attacker cannot replace its identity, so R3’s measured advantage lies in the
replacement channel. The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, the
evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents that never
replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C∗ was selected in development on gross
efficiency in effect.

